Make the task count a parameter of the function, not of the machine, then loop over it in the test. This tests the chunking logic even when Julia runs on one thread.

```julia
using Test

function chunked_sum(xs; ntasks)
    chunks = Iterators.partition(eachindex(xs), cld(length(xs), ntasks))
    return sum(fetch, map(idx -> Threads.@spawn(sum(view(xs, idx))), chunks))
end

xs = collect(1:1000)
@testset "ntasks = $n" for n in (1, 2, 4, 7)
    @test chunked_sum(xs; ntasks = n) == sum(xs)
end

fs = [sin(i) for i in 1:1000]
@testset "float ntasks = $n" for n in (1, 2, 4, 7)
    @test chunked_sum(fs; ntasks = n) ≈ sum(fs)
end
```

Integers are compared exactly, floats with `≈`, because different chunking changes the summation order. Also run the whole suite under `julia -t 1` and a multi-thread setting in CI.
